art for the  black community that is reliving  this on television every day since  monday  and we don't know how long this trial is  going to pull off those wounds  so it hurts but i would like to say  and i'll be the first to start to repent  i'm sorry to the black community for  what they're suffering  i'm sorry sheriff for what happened to  shea  and his family and by the way shea's  stepfather  was one of the very first captains in  the boldenary command  that helped us with safe village and my  right pastor cherry  and served the black community and  that's his son who's in the hospital  paralyzed  i'm sorry sheriff that that happened i'm  sorry to all the police officers  i'm sorry to these young people that for  some reason we can't get it together  after 400 years  but i figure if we start to say i'm  sorry and start to repent  then maybe we can start to reform  and we've been in this mac community and  i've been on it for about 15 years  month after month and there has been  many strides which you're going to hear  about  from some great people and i'm going to  let them share about that  but what i'd like to do now is pray and  ask god  to help us and i'm going to read this  scripture  the lord said after 400 years  i have seen how cruel my people are  being treated  i have heard them cry out to be rescued  from their oppressors and i know all  about their sufferings  god said that at one time for a people  that had been oppressed for  400 years coincidentally  the other thing is i like us to know  that the bible says  clearly that we were all created  in the image of god and it doesn't say  what color  we were all created in the image of god  so saying those two scriptures i like to  bow our heads  and let us pray lord we come before you  right now we thank you for your mercy  and your grace and your patience with us  as a people  as a nation we come before you lord i  ask for  law enforcement to be lifted up before  you for wisdom  guidance protection for their families  i pray for our sheriff and all of the  staff here lord that you give them  wisdom and understanding how to work  with the community  i lift up the black community i lift up  every race right now lord  i specifically pray for these young  people that they will see this turning  point  let las vegas be the place where you  smile upon us where you help us  where you hear this cry of repentance  and you help us to change things that's  what reform means  and i pray this in the name of the  father in the name of your son jesus  and the holy spirit and i thank you and  everyone that would agree would say  amen thank you  good afternoon everyone i am dora  legrand and before  i uh begin i have to say to the pastor i  hope it's not just  the black community that is hurting  because before he was formed in his  father's belly  and sanctified in his mother's womb he  was a human being  and so it is humans that was on the  ground  not just a black man so yes black  community is hurting but i hope that  people  who love people all over this world are  hurting as well  we as a nation as a community we're at  an inflection point right now  and metro as the sheriff said in an  article that he did back on march 12  has had not only metro but all of the  police departments across this community  have been affected affected by what took  place and it caused  change we had some things going on here  and so we worked hard  metro listened they instituted ethics  guidelines  and in those ethics guidelines it's one  thing to talk about the sanctity of  human life  but it's another thing it's another  thing to actually work towards  maintaining human life  that's what was put in the ethics  portion of the  use of force policy then you talk about  the de-escalation aspect  if you want to do anything when you're  trying to  arrest somebody you've got to  de-escalate the situation just so you  can bring some  statisticity to it you have to do that  then you talk about  putting them in a recovery position you  can't do anything when you have somebody  down with their face in the concrete  you can't talk they're going to listen  they're going to hear anything that you  have to say  and you have to call medical attention  and so we worked on those things we  worked really hard on those things  and those things um  were in custody incidents that we saw  play out not only with children there  were some things here  that took place as well that made us  really uncomfortable the fact that metro  will stand and listen  is important what impressed me more than  anything else and and i  am i consider myself to be the devil's  advocate on the mac committee  because that's the only way that you're  going to generate debate  that's the only way you're going to make  people step up that's the only way  you're going to make people change  is to challenge the system and to  challenge the status quo  and that's typically what we do and so  when  while we're doing this what they did on  september i think it was about the 20th  or the 25th  they came back with an after action  review of everything that took place  from march until june of last year i was  really impressed they did a  very thorough swat analysis i'm a  detailed person so the detail was  important to me  they did did a very thorough swallow  analysis and the sheriff said a couple  of weeks ago  we didn't do anything wrong i'm going to  beg to differ with them you did  something wrong but the fact of the  matter is you made a decision  to correct the wrongs and to do them  right and that's what's important  so we have a lot to look forward to as  we look at this trial every day  it hurts you to your heart to see what  is taking place  we have to make sure that as a community  we come  to this law enforcement we hold this law  enforcement accountable  and one of the things that i'm going to  i want to encourage us to do as a  community  is for the young people to get involved  we have a mac committee whose average  age is  probably about 50 right huh  well maybe um and that's that's fine  but at the end of the day it's the alex  bernal the craig knight summit  even though he's 50. some of the younger  ones we need you guys to come and get  involved you can sit there it's one  thing  to talk about a problem it's another  thing to help solve it  and if you aren't doing anything to help  solve it then you're a part of it  and we want to um ask you to please be  civil  let the trial play out i i'm probably  less hopeful than anyone  but at the at the very same time we need  to work within our own community  if we effectuate change here one teaches  one and who knows how far  that will go so thank you  good afternoon everyone peter guzman  president of the latin chamber of  commerce  i like what my colleague and friend dora  said about it's not just one group's  hurting it's everybody that's hurting  the latin chamber of commerce hurts with  our community  but those hurting also include law  enforcement  the latin chamber and myself we always  stand with law enforcement  because we know that there are men and  women  that while we're sleeping they're  protecting our community keeping our  community  as together as they possibly can so  they're hurting as well  so we all hurt together as a community  and while we watched and continue to  watch this trial  that's another place but we're here this  is our community  we have seen tragedy after tragedy here  and we've always come together  as a community we need to continue to do  that what i can share with you is that  i'm honored to be on  mac and i'm honored to be on it because  it's a real group of folks that are  in the trenches trying to make law  enforcement a little better  and that includes our sheriff we have  heated debates in this in this group  we're allowed to say whatever we want to  say  because we're trying to accomplish one  thing and that is a community  a community that's safer better  and it takes all of us to do that so i'm  privileged to be  a small part of that community and i  will continue to do what i can  to make sure that we're doing it a  little better  every day so thank you very much  good afternoon my name is roxanne mccoy  i am the president of the las vegas  naacp  and i would like to first give thanks to  our sheriff  for being an amazing community partner  to not only myself  but to the las vegas community as well  as the african-american community  and my mac members brothers and sisters  that stand with me and stand behind me  right now to support the efforts of  making sure  justice matters for all of us i would  like to start off by speaking on behalf  of the relationship between  the lvmpd um and  the naacp and the murder child  of george floyd at the death at the  hands  of a fire loss at the hands of a fired  police officer a little background on  lbnpd and the las vegas naacp  in 2018 2019 we received several calls  regarding the use of force in las vegas  before the death of george floyd we had  an  in-custody death of a gentleman by the  name of byron williams here locally  in our city it gave us an opportunity to  take a look at  our local use of force policy here in  las vegas  with that we have i had the opportunity  to have a conversation with our share  and talk to him about some of the  concerns that were noticed  in the videos from that in custody death  and with that the sheriff listened to  the concerns and those were concerns  that came from the african-american  community  community as well as other community  members  and we looked and we decided that there  was some changes that could be made  the sheriff with his leadership asked if  we could  work with our mac committee in which we  have formed here with us today  we all together took a look at those  policies and worked with our police  department in order to change  those policies for the betterment of our  community as a whole  we worked on some of the things that my  colleagues talked about earlier  was de-escalation the chokehold  the duty to intervene medical attention  the appropriateness of force and body  cam usage  and as we look at the trial that's  coming up or that's taking place right  now  we will see a lot of those same things  that are on trial right now today  the work was tough but the naacp  has a good respectful partnership with  our lvmpd  who was willing to sit down at the table  with us to have the tough conversations  in that for in an effort to change what  our community looks like  what it could look like and what we want  it to look like  with the upcoming trial we all witnessed  and saw that took place firsthand  the nation as well as our community  should watch listen  and wait to see if justice will be  served for our country  and our community as we witness this  process  i would ask all of us to maintain a  peaceful  a peaceful posture while these outcomes  are determined i will caution us all not  to respond irrationally  and cause more harm for ourselves we  have seen some people protest and it's  called exercising their rights  but when black people do the same it's  looked upon differently or called riots  we are all cautious and frustrated with  the system that we have known it to be  however let us try to be patient  maintain control in our response so that  we do not become part of a broken system  that will continue to victimize us this  is a slow moving process  but we in clark county have made  significant change  in our city and we don't want to undo  all the work that has been done  thus far we have all been frustrated in  2020 and 2021  about this egregious act that allowed  the world to see  what black mothers and families have  dealt with for years  justice matters health care matters  people matter  and black lives matter thank you  [Applause]  good afternoon my name is craig knight  general manager for kcep 88.1 fm  which is also known as the people  station and we have a slogan that we say  that we are one and the reason  reason why we say we are one is because  we are  we are all human beings first and  in order to get along we have to  dialogue and communicate  and being in radio that's what we do  best about eight years ago  i was recruited to be a member of this  mac committee  and at first i was hesitant but then  when i thought about it i said you know  the only way  to salvage relationships  and to better them is to have dialogue  and communication  so i joined mujihe ramadan and the mac  committee  and i found that it was very useful  and a great tool as far as bringing  understanding  to the community because we all  understand that we know  there's been this great divide and not  so great relationship  between law enforcement and community so  i felt  that being a part of mac knowing  that we can be a part of the policy  um encouraging and policy changes  that will prevent a lot of incidents and  issues that happens  in other cities that's one of the main  reasons why i joined and i see  that a lot of the policies  that we talk about  and collaborate on  turns out that every time we come up  with something  it always happens before something else  happens in another city  with george floyd for instance when that  happened  the use of force policy body cams  and other policies were already put in  place proactively  before that happened so  it's a testament to see that being  proactive and having a relationship  you can actually prevent things from  happening  before they happen and we also see that  the relationship that we have with the  sheriff  in metro in that committee and the radio  station  that we found that we're bringing  understanding to the communities we're  bringing  understanding to the point to where  we're all here to try to make a  difference for the betterment  of everyone but the only way to do that  you have to dialogue so i commend  sheriff  and his entire staff for  having us here monthly to communicate  and have an open mind to listen  to what is going on in community and  what people are thinking  and what we can do to fix it and better  the relationship  between law enforcement and community  and lastly i also like to say that  we appreciate metro because they sponsor  a radio show on 88.1 every wednesday  at 9 30 a.m called police promoting  unity  that's an opportunity to have a a  platform and a voice  from both sides to communicate and  dialogue  to bring together more of an  understanding of what we're trying to do  what we need to do and what we will we  want to do  in order to move  forward the relationship between law  enforcement and community because that's  to me that's very important we have to  have understanding between the two and i  think  what the mac committee at metro is doing  that i'm a part of  we're doing that so i just want to say  thank you sheriff  and thank you mac committee  all right thank you craig knight my name  is jose solorio  i'm a proud son of mexican immigrant  since the first latino to serve in the  clark county school board a while back  and uh i've been serving on this  committee through  three sheriffs keller sheriff gillespie  and sheriff lombardo  this committee has been effective in  communicating the needs  of the different minorities communities  within this town  and that has made a difference it's  taken a while but it's made a difference  and the reason i say that is because las  vegas  is not like other cities las vegas is  progressive  las vegas listens to its communities  so what has happened because they've  been listening we've had policies that  have been adopted by  metro we meet once a month and these  meetings aren't always cordial  they're very intense sometimes but the  purpose is for the sheriff to listen to  the communities to see how our community  community could be served better by  metro and one of the first things that  we talked about back in the 90s was  our police force needs to look like the  community  and i'm happy to report that that is  happening now  as you can tell by our young cadets  or young people behind me our  next line of officers looks more and  more like our community  so that's the first step the second step  we did was  on use of force i call it de-escalation  nowadays if you get stopped by a police  officer you're more likely to have a  positive encounter  about the issue at hand there's no time  to fear the police  the police is us we are the community  we meet once a month with the sheriff we  get things done  and we are different than other  departments  within our united states and i'm proud  to be on this committee and serve with  sheriff joe  gillespie i mean sorry  joe lombardo he went to the same high  school i did  alex bernal clark county um  everyone said some amazing words i'm  gonna be real quick this will be the  fastest 20 minutes  you've ever heard just playing  um i grew up in north las vegas i was  once an at-risk youth i was all i worked  with gangs for almost 20 years  and then i changed my career and i'm  working on recreation as uh working the  recreation senior center  i got a call some years back from mr  ramadan who said hey would you  like to help me work with the rookie  academy  metro academy and start working with  them on how to connect with the  community  how the officers can go out there and  start just get out of their car and just  start  making that connection a young man like  me from north las vegas who had no goals  no aspirations didn't know what he can  do with his life  but just the thinking there was a time  when i didn't like the police officers  because i was on the other side and now  i appreciate them more as i get older  and i'm paying my taxes  and i have kids and i have i want to i  want to contribute  but the fact that mr ramadan saw  something in me and say hey would you  come help  just talk to the officers well that led  to me not only that but i had the  opportunity prior to that  i used to work for the attorney general  of texas i used to train all the gang  officers back in texas  my job was to help them to communicate  and the fact that we're doing it here  also the fact that they took that upon  they're like hey alex we want you to be  involved with this we want you to help  that means a lot you know a lot of  officers can they can say we know what  we're doing this is what we do because  they used to tell me that the two  hardest people to ever  train are teachers and officers one  thinks they know it all and the other  ones  thinks they've seen it all these  officers here  and the sheriff here they're willing to  accept that they want to they want to  learn they want to get better and i love  that being part of the mac  just the fact that i can sit there and  listen to these people  and also they value my opinion again i  have to remember i'm just a kid from  north las vegas who went to rancho  and but it just makes me feel good and  know that  information that i get here is valued  but i can also get information and bring  it back to the community and also they  embrace that  so i thank you sheriff for allowing me  to be part of this mac and thank you mr  ramadan also thank you  [Applause]  are you guys wore out yet all right i  want everybody to come on in here real  close  i want all my devil devil advocates real  close  and i'm going to build on that  and dora get in here i want you right  next to me  all right just don't hurt me i'm not  going to hurt you  so as you can tell there's somebody  missing today  and that's representatives from the city  in the county government  this is not a government press  conference i did not call this press  conference  i actually had to ask to participate in  this press conference  it was requested by mr mujahid ramadan  as the chairman of the mac committee  to formulate a press conference and he  figured if i promoted it people would  show up  and uh i'm glad you did and the  important piece 